  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0d/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_49.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Angel takes a shortcut over the factory of Tony Stark, but he sees Iron Man below, waving at him.&nbsp; A deadly explosion is about to be tested, and Iron Man is trying to warn him off, but it's too late -- the explosion rocks the scene, and while Iron Man has his armor to protect him, the Angel is fully caught by the blast, and the ensuing radiation changes him.&nbsp; It turns him evil, and he takes off!&nbsp; Iron Man sees the change and pursues, but his boot jets run out, and he has to make an emergency landing, slowing his fall with the aid of his \"magnetic repellers,\" allowing the Angel to escape.\n\nSoon at the X-Men headquarters, Angel announces that he's quitting the team!&nbsp; He's going to go find the evil mutants and join them.&nbsp; The other X-Men try but fail to stop him.&nbsp; When Professor X returns, he admits the situation is serious enough to call the Avengers for help.&nbsp; But the Hulk, Thor, Giant-Man and the Wasp are all occupied, and only Iron Man receives the call.&nbsp; He feels responsible for the Angel's turn and decides to handle this by himself.\n\nAngel is out hunting for the evil mutants, making a ruckus to be noticed by them.&nbsp; However, \"the wary evil mutants, recognizing the Angel, suspect a <i>trap</i> and do <i>not</i> show themselves!\"&nbsp; But Iron Man heads for the scene.&nbsp; The two scuffle and evade each other, Iron Man not fully appreciating the extent of Angel's personality shift.&nbsp; He still thinks that somehow Angel hasn't fully turned bad, so he jets toward him and grabs, pulling them both as high as his boot jets will carry them.&nbsp; They eventually sputter out, and Iron Man begins to fall.&nbsp; He's now completely at Angel's mercy.\n\nIron Man plummets and realizes there's nothing he can do to save himself.&nbsp; Angel stares at him, knowing he has no chance.&nbsp; But... he can't do it!&nbsp; Angel swoops down and rescues Iron Man, proving to everyone that he's <i>not</i> actually evil.&nbsp; The shock of the experience finally opened the Angel's eyes as to what happened -- the atomic rays \"affected his brain for a time...until <i>now</i>!\"&nbsp; Iron Man risked his life to shock him back to normal.\n\nThe other X-Men arrive on the scene to take Angel home, and the Professor telepathically reaches out to thank Iron Man for risking his life for one of his X-Men and offers his help, should he ever need it.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Readable enough, with a couple of glimmers that break through the haze.\nThe rest is functional, forgettable, and carried more by curiosity than craft.",
  "subtitle": "When Your Nuclear Blast Needs a Second Draft",
  "review_text": "Considering how many times this tale was referenced over the years [DECODER_RING: And reprinted in 1975's Giant-Size Iron Man #1], to finally read it was something I had never done.&nbsp; And once I did, the first thing that struck me was how casually the script treats something that should be catastrophic.\n\n[ASSET: left | NuclearExplosion | width=24%] The personality-altering plot device was just as uninspired here as it was in the previous <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> issue that put Thor side-by-side with Loki (in May's [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94|2026-09-29]).\n[BR]\n[BR] But what was even <i>more</i> baffling was Tony Stark staging an \"atomic explosion\" at his weapons plant, which resulted in a \"highly-refined nuclear explosion.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Pardon me for shaking my head at the sheer irresponsibility of doing this in a populated area.&nbsp; That <i>alone</i> should be reason for concern!\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Are we sure the Angel was the only one whose reasoning was affected here?]\n\nNot only that, but you'd think a \"nuclear explosion\" would have <b>laid waste</b> to Stark's plant, but when Iron Man falls out of the sky after his boot jets run out of power on page 4, he falls through the roof of a building below which has the word <b>STARK</b> on it.\n\nI'm sorry, but [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=where is the devastation?]&nbsp; How did this building survive the explosion?&nbsp; In fact, there seems to be no evidence at <i>all</i> that an atomic blast has been set off!\n\nWas Stark testing a new weapon that affects personality but leaves buildings intact?&nbsp; The narrative never even gestures toward an explanation, and yet the whole story <b>hinges</b> on the fact that Angel's personality was altered by a [SPAN: severity=error; text=horrific explosion] -- one that requires the power of the Iron Man armor to protect Tony Stark, yet otherwise seems to leave behind no additional collateral damage!&nbsp; This robs the event of any dramatic weight -- the story treats a nuclear blast like a minor inconvenience.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Is this what a \"highly-refined nuclear explosion\" means in the narration on page 2?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(There seems to be no other alternative!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIt was nice seeing the characters crossover of course -- knowing that the X-Men and Avengers are all around and could appear at any moment in each other's books.&nbsp; But outside of that, the story did little to elevate itself.\n\n[ASSET: left | Avengers | width=21%] [ASSET: right | GiantManAndWasp | width=20%] Page 8 implies that the Hulk is still considered to be a member of the Avengers, even though he quit at the end of [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08] (published only last month), making the implication here feel oddly out of step with what readers just saw.\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, that same page states that Hank Pym and Janet Van Dyne are at a \"rare social evening together\" and \"only have eyes and thoughts for each other!\"&nbsp; [BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Whoa!]&nbsp; That's a <i>completely</i> different impression than what we get in the various issues of <i>Tales to Astonish</i>, where it seems Hank has been trying hard to keep things cool between them!&nbsp; So this sudden romantic framing lands with a thud.\n\n[ASSET: left | PepperAndHappy | width=22%] I liked the fact that, even in the midst of the other action, the creators take a moment to spotlight the whole Happy Hogan/Pepper Potts dynamic.&nbsp; Even at this early stage in their development, it already seems obvious that the pair are crazy about each other, but Pepper in particular hides this by constantly hurling insults in Happy's direction.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's charming, but surprisingly <i>nuanced</i> for a book that otherwise leans heavily on broad strokes.&nbsp; And in this case, it's one of the few moments that gives the issue a sense of lived‑in continuity that has little to do with the featured main story.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe action sequences that compose the bulk of pages 11-17 (and mostly consist of the airborne battle between the two heroes) aren't visually stimulating.&nbsp; The layouts rarely vary, and the aerial choreography never quite sells the danger, but they do the job to keep the story readable.\n\n[ASSET: left | ProfessorXThanksIronMan | width=12%] In the end, though, it should surprise no one that Angel snaps out of his momentary madness in time to save the armored Avenger, he returns to the X-Men; Professor X thanks Iron Man and offers his assistance, should it ever be needed, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved].\n[BR]\n[BR] It's readable enough... but otherwise antiseptic and bland.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | Doohickeys | width=10%] As if the story being weak wasn't bad enough, Stan Lee's normally good grasp of dialogue suffers when Tony Stark calls his armor a \"contraption,\" himself an \"ever-lovin' millionaire playboy industrialist,\" and explains that the easiest way to remove his armor is to \"release all the magnetic doohickeys and the whole caboodle snaps neatly off and folds into place!\"\n\nIt's not that the armor's features aren't <i>nice</i> (because they <i>are</i>), but is this really the way Tony Stark speaks?&nbsp; I seriously doubt it!&nbsp; This is supposed to be the head of a weapons manufacturing company, and Stan has him speaking like just another teenager in the X-Men!&nbsp; <b><i>Doohickeys?</i></b>  [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Hard to read...]\n\nAs for the artwork, it's worth pointing out that the cover (drawn by Kirby and inked by Ditko) shows the Iron Man armor looking <i>far</i> more like metal than Ditko's rendition on the inner pages, which continue to make it appear more like [SPAN: severity=error; text=cloth pajamas] than anything else.\n\n[ASSET: left | ScrawnyIronMan | width=13%] It's <i>true</i> that Paul Reinman's inking <i>does</i> help mitigate Steve's tendency to make characters look balloon-like (and definitely is an improvement over what <i>might</i> have appeared if Ditko had inked his own pencils), but it's <i>also </i>true that Iron Man looks scrawny and unimpressive in this story.\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, he doesn't even seem <i>larger</i> than the Angel, who is still a teenager.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Really?]\n\nI freely admit that the red-and-gold armor Steve designed last month is nice ([SPAN: severity=success; text=and I <i>like</i> it a lot]), but there is no <i>question</i> in my mind that artist Don Heck was [SPAN: severity=info; text=far better suited for this magazine], and just like with [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6], assigning Ditko to this book seems like an obvious mismatch.\n\nMy preference is to [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"Bring back Don Heck!\"] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Please?)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=But on the positive side], Steve and Paul manage to pull off a believable set of X-Men.&nbsp; In fact, the mutant team of teenagers <i>dominates</i> the midsection of the book, taking up the <i>entirety</i> of pages 5, 6, most of 7, and half of 8 (with the futile call out to the Avengers rounding out the <i>remainder</i> of that page).\n\nWhat [SPAN: severity=error; text=<i>wasn't</i> as believable] was the fact that on page 3, Iron Man already senses that the Angel's personality has changed, but still waits until the X-Men call out for help before deciding to go after him (because he felt responsible).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Why did he wait?]\n[BR] It's hard to reconcile his early alarm with his later passivity.\n\nAnother point of contention is the statement on the top of page 11 which suggests that \"the wary evil mutants\" recognized the Angel and did not believe he wasn't trying to lure them out, suspecting a <i>trap</i>.&nbsp; But, since the <i>X-Men</i> has published just two issues at this point, featuring only Magneto ([LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25]) and the Vanisher ([LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17], who was rendered harmless by Professor X at its end), we may fairly ask:\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=Exactly who <i>are</i> these \"evil mutants\" that suspected a trap?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The script is asking readers to accept a threat that simply doesn't exist yet due to the lack of enough villains to justify the claim.)]\n\nWhat seems far more likely is that, in Stan's rush to feature one of the X-Men outside the pages of the actual <i>X-Men</i> magazine, he trades narrative cohesion for spectacle by showing two of Marvel's heroes pitted against each other.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But... <i>small mercies</i>... at <i>least</i> it wasn't the Puppet Master's doing!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMarvel obviously felt they had some major copyright issues to contend with, since the X-Men and the Avengers both appear courtesy of a \"special arrangement\" by the publishers of those magazines, and then we turn the page to the backup feature, only to find the Watcher appearing thanks to the <i>Fantastic Four</i> magazine.\n\nThere are guest stars [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=all over the place] and other magazines seemingly waiting in line to demand their share of the profits!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(You just have to laugh!)]\n\nAs for the hero, this story makes Iron Man appear far weaker than what most readers would otherwise assume.&nbsp; For example, page 13 shows Iron Man going to <i>extra effort</i> just to summon the strength to tear open a steel door of the airplane hangar, even though previous issues (see [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41]) showed him catching... and <b><i>crushing</i></b>... a cannonball, albeit in his old, all-golden armor.\n\nHis boot jets <i>also</i> seem frail in this issue, lasting only a short time.&nbsp; This is inconsistent with what the magazine has shown before, namely, [LINK:1963:TOS_42_iron0|TOS #42], which showed him flying \"to the nearest western country\" from behind the Iron Curtain on its final page.&nbsp; ([SPAN: severity=success; text=Though to be entirely fair,] [LINK:1963:AVEN_1|AVEN #1|2026-11-13] had him using his solar battery on its page 6 to fly cross-country before switching over to his boot jets -- all in an effort to conserve energy.)\n\nIt's only an impression, but it just strikes me that Iron Man should have had no trouble dealing with someone like the Angel, whose only power is flight.&nbsp; One would think this should be one of the [SPAN: severity=info; text=least challenging opponents] he's faced so far.\n\nWe might quickly try and blame it on the new armor --  which <i>could</i> be partially responsible -- perhaps postulating that it simply needs some additional fine tuning.&nbsp; That's all well and good, but last issue's page 8 unequivocally stated that \"this new iron armor is every bit as powerful!\"&nbsp; The downgrade we see here seems so sharp that it feels like a <i>regression</i> rather than a recalibration.\n\nBut... let's be real... he's still breaking it in, and if this issue proved anything, it's that\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=there are still some bugs to work out.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn the end, Stan's double-dip (again, see [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94|2026-09-29]) into the narrative cheat of personality-altering -- thanks to a gratuitous (and <i>very</i> feeble) \"nuclear explosion\" that seems to have caused [SPAN: severity=warning; text=no visible damage] -- is his way of kick-starting a conflict between one of the X-Men and one of the Avengers.&nbsp; His choice of the Angel is head-scratching, as I might have chosen a more powerful member of the X-Men (perhaps Cyclops) to face off with Iron Man.\n\nAside from the whole [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"I am now evil\"] aspect, the story and art were perfunctory, but never rise above functional.&nbsp; Even so, it provided a way to shove the X-Men in the faces of the reading audience, and [SPAN: severity=info; text=from a business standpoint], spotlighting the X‑Men made perfect sense.\n\nFinally, the Watcher's use as narrator in the <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> backup tale called \"The Saga of the Sneepers\" is notable enough to earn the book extra credit, not because he actually <i>does</i> anything in the story (because he doesn't), but because he's able to share information with the reader about events transpiring elsewhere.\n\nIt's less about plot contribution and more about the novelty of his narrative presence, and so his use -- along with his unique perspective -- is worthy of a [SPAN: severity=success; text=one-time, half-point bonus].&nbsp; His presence gives the book a scope it otherwise lacks (even when one takes into account all the copyright notices, <i>heh</i>).",
  "side_note": [
    "[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=Tales of the Watcher]\n\n[ASSET: left | TalesOfTheWatcher | width=20%] In this brief feature, the Watcher makes only his third appearance (after [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13] and [LINK:1963:FF_20|FF #20|2026-12-25]) and serves as narrator, in this case introducing readers to the \"Sneepers,\" a conquering race which is keeping a close eye on Earth at a distance, concerned that earthlings continue to make advancement after advancement.&nbsp; They wonder if earthlings will eventually become strong enough to conquer <i>them</i> instead of vice versa.\n[BR]\n[BR] In the end, after observing that the people of Earth appear to be threatening each <b><i>other</i></b>, they elect to do nothing at all!\n\nThe Watcher then addresses the reader directly and points out that \"the Sneepers wait for your self-destruction!&nbsp; But you must <i>not</i> allow it to happen!\"&nbsp; He goes on to tell readers that only <i>they</i> have the power to prove the Sneepers wrong -- [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=\"You must heed my warning!\"]\n\nAs for him, he can only observe -- he has no power to <i>act</i>... \"for I am... <b><i>the Watcher</i></b>!\"\n\nUnquestionably, the ending lands a bit on the [SPAN: severity=info; text=preachy side], but the direct callout by the Watcher gives Stan a veritable soapbox upon which he can leap to make sweeping observations about the state of humanity.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And <i>nowhere</i> is this more pronounced than in the still-to-come <b><i>Silver Surfer</i></b> title of the later 1960s!]\n\nBut rather than elaborate more on this as part of one issue's review, I've elected to hold back most of my commentary until this month's summary (which is next in the reading order), so check that out."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "Iron Man's boot jets and power levels are still a pale imitation of what they eventually will become, and he runs out of power far too many times, but obviously his armor is constantly being upgraded.&nbsp; At this point, he can hardly be considered the second most powerful Avenger, as he later becomes, second only to Thor.\n\nRefer to <i>Iron Man</i> 114 (IRON #114) for a fantastic statement to this effect -- also Avengers Annual #8 (1978).",
  "highlight": "A personality‑warped Angel tangles with Iron Man, while the X‑Men appear just long enough to wonder why Stark's explosions only affect flying teenagers -- and no buildings."
}